{
  "posts": [
    {
      "title": "Your Villages Weekend Recap: Rocky, Jazz & a Warm One",
      "date": "June 29, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
      "body": "<p>Well, hello again. Pull up a chair. I hope you got out this weekend, because the squares were humming. It was a warm one &mdash; central Florida in late June, you know the drill &mdash; but the music didn't mind one bit. Here's the scoop on what I caught.</p>\n\n<h3>The squares were singing</h3>\n<p>Friday night I made my way down to <strong>Lake Sumter Landing</strong>, and who do I find but <strong>Rocky and The Rollers</strong> &mdash; 27 years of doing this in The Villages and still packing the dance floor. They closed out the weekend Sunday night too. If you like your oldies high-energy with a little doo-wop sparkle, those are your guys. Saturday the Landing switched it up with <strong>Osaka Fall</strong>, who run everything from old-school jams to glam rock and soulful slow ones. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lake Sumter Landing schedule</a>)</p>\n<p>Over at <strong>Sawgrass Grove</strong>, Friday belonged to <strong>3P</strong>, that acoustic trio that bounces between reggae, country and pop without missing a step. Saturday brought in <strong>Radlin' Rootz</strong>, an eclectic band of top-tier players out of Ocala. Those same Rootz folks turned up Sunday at <strong>Spanish Springs</strong> &mdash; so if you missed them one night, the square gods gave you a second chance. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/sawgrass-grove/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sawgrass Grove</a>, <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/spanish-springs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Spanish Springs</a>)</p>\n<p>And <strong>Brownwood</strong> kept the Saturday crowd on their feet with a Motown-and-Top-40 set built for dancing. The squares run music every single night, 6 to 10, and it's free &mdash; still the best deal in town. (<a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/category/town-square-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Live Square Entertainment</a>)</p>\n\n<h3>A little something indoors, too</h3>\n<p>Not everyone wants to sweat through a square dance in June, and I don't blame you. Saturday night, <strong>Savannah Center</strong> hosted <strong>3DN &mdash; The Ultimate 3 Dog Nite Tribute Show</strong>. If &ldquo;Joy to the World&rdquo; is still on your car radio, that was the ticket. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesdailysun.com/calendar/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Daily Sun calendar</a>)</p>\n<p>Here's my who-knew note for the weekend. Sunday evening, <strong>More Jazz</strong> brought the <strong>Rick Steuart Quartet</strong> to <strong>Colony Cottage Recreation Center</strong>. A quiet, classy night of jazz tucked into a rec center &mdash; that's the kind of thing folks live here for years and never try. Trust me on this one: the smaller rec-center shows are some of the best-kept secrets we've got. (<a href=\"https://www.eventbrite.com/d/fl--the-villages/events/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">events listing</a>)</p>\n\n<h3>Eating around the music</h3>\n<p>I won't pretend I tracked down a brand-new restaurant opening this weekend &mdash; I didn't, and I won't make one up for you. But the squares make their own dinner plan. Grab a bite at the Landing or Sawgrass before the band starts, find a spot with a little shade, and let the night come to you. That's the move when it's this warm out.</p>\n<p>Speaking of warm &mdash; let's be honest about it. We sat in the low-to-mid 90s all weekend with the usual afternoon thunderstorms rolling through. (<a href=\"https://www.accuweather.com/en/us/the-villages/32159/weather-forecast/2257923\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AccuWeather</a>) Keep water in the cart, find the late-day breeze, and don't fight the heat &mdash; outlast it. The music doesn't get going till the sun's on its way down anyway.</p>\n\n<h3>Looking ahead</h3>\n<p>If you're already itching for the week, <strong>1776 the Musical</strong> takes the stage at Savannah Center tonight, Monday, at 7. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesdailysun.com/calendar/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Daily Sun calendar</a>) A little patriotic warm-up before the Fourth.</p>\n<p>That's what I could confirm from a warm, music-filled weekend. Quieter on the big-event front, but the squares carried it like they always do. Now go enjoy yourself.</p>\n<p>See you around the square,<br>&mdash; Susan</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Villages Weekend + What's New: Rocky's Back on the Square",
      "date": "June 25, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
      "body": "<p>Here's the scoop, friends. The squares are humming, a fresh beach bar just opened, and the summer hours mean the music starts a little later and runs a little longer. Pour something cold and let me walk you through your weekend.</p><p>Quick note before we dance: through August, nightly music on the squares runs <strong>6 to 10 p.m.</strong>, not the old 5 to 9. Happy Hour still holds at 5 to 6. So you've got time for dinner first. I'm already planning my Saturday around it. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/nightly-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Villages Entertainment</a>)</p><h3>What to do this weekend</h3><p><strong>Friday night on the squares.</strong> Take your pick. Spanish Springs has <strong>Fancy Reagan</strong>, Lake Sumter Landing turns into a dance party with <strong>CLUB CTRL featuring DJ Lu-S</strong>, and Brownwood brings <strong>The Brothers Jukebox</strong>. All free, all 6 to 10. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/nightly-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">square schedule</a>) My tip: eat early at Lake Sumter, grab a patio table by the water, then drift toward the music. Those waterfront tables fill up fast once the band starts.</p><p><strong>Saturday is the big one.</strong> A real Villages favorite is back at Lake Sumter Landing &mdash; <strong>Rocky and The Rollers</strong>, 6 to 10. Twenty-seven years playing here and they still pack the square. Trust me on this one, get there early for a spot. Over at Spanish Springs you'll find <strong>Gravity Parade</strong>, and Brownwood has <strong>The Fire Rhythms Band</strong>. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/nightly-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">square schedule</a>)</p><p>Want a seat and air conditioning? <strong>3DN &mdash; The Ultimate 3 Dog Nite Tribute</strong> plays the Savannah Center on Saturday, 7 to 8:30 p.m. They run through all 21 of Three Dog Night's Top 40 hits &mdash; &ldquo;Joy to the World,&rdquo; &ldquo;Mama Told Me Not to Come,&rdquo; &ldquo;Shambala,&rdquo; the whole songbook. Tickets start around $35. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/ticketed-performances/3dn-the-three-dog-night-experience/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">show details</a>) I sent my own neighbor to their last one and she came home singing.</p><p><strong>Sunday, ease into it.</strong> Lake Sumter Landing has <strong>The Dave Capp Project</strong>, Spanish Springs the <strong>Justin Heet Band</strong>, and Brownwood winds down with <strong>5.0.1.</strong> playing blues and soul, all 6 to 10. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/nightly-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">square schedule</a>) And if you like your evening smoother, <strong>More Jazz presents the Rick Steuart Quartet</strong> at Colony Cottage Recreation Center, starting 6 p.m. (<a href=\"https://allevents.in/the-villages/music\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">event listing</a>) A glass of wine and a little jazz on a Sunday &mdash; you're welcome.</p><p><strong>Where to eat near the music.</strong> Lake Sumter is my easy answer because you can stroll right from dinner to the band. If you're at Brownwood, do an early bite and stay for the show. And here's a happy thought for Saturday morning &mdash; the Brownwood Farmers Market runs on the square, so you can grab fresh finds before the heat sets in. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/0626_PAHCalendar__BW.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood June calendar</a>)</p><h3>What's new</h3><p><strong>A new beach bar in Middleton.</strong> Say hello to <strong>Zunzibar</strong>, now open in downtown Middleton on the waterfront. It's a laid-back, beachy spot serving African, Caribbean, and Mediterranean flavors &mdash; think award-winning wings, fresh seafood, and tropical cocktails, with that big downtown water view. (<a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/20/new-restaurant-opening-monday-in-middleton/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Villages-News</a> &middot; <a href=\"https://mymiddleton.com/news/zunzibar-update\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Middleton</a>) I haven't been yet, so I won't oversell it &mdash; but a frozen drink by the water in June? I'll be finding out soon. Tell 'em Susan sent you.</p><p><strong>The new Walmart is open.</strong> The big <strong>Walmart on County Road 466 in Lady Lake</strong> opened its doors Wednesday, and folks were lined up early. (<a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/24/early-birds-out-in-force-for-opening-of-new-walmart/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Villages-News</a>) Not glamorous, I know, but a closer grocery run is a closer grocery run. Worth knowing which side of town you're on now.</p><p><strong>A second ALDI is coming.</strong> The discount grocer announced an opening date for its <strong>second Villages-area store at La Plaza Grande</strong>. (<a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/05/30/opening-date-announced-for-second-aldi-store-in-the-villages/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Villages-News</a>) If you're an ALDI quarter-for-the-cart person like me, this one's good news.</p><p>That's the honest list this week. I only put it in if I could check it, so if your favorite event isn't here, it just means I couldn't confirm the date in time &mdash; not that nothing's happening. When in doubt, I leave it out.</p><p>So that's your weekend. Music every night, a new spot to try, and Rocky and The Rollers to get you off your chair Saturday. Save me a seat by the water.</p><p>See you around the square,<br>&mdash; Susan</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Villages Weekend Recap: Music, Bingo & a Cold One",
      "date": "June 22, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
      "body": "<p>Well, friends, we made it through another hot one. Here's the scoop on the weekend that just wrapped, and trust me, the squares did not disappoint.</p><p>Friday night I had a choice to make, and so did you. Every square had a band going at 6:00. Over at <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/18/live-square-entertainment-for-friday-june-19/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood Paddock Square</a>, it was <strong>Eighteen87</strong> cranking out the hits. Lake Sumter Landing had <strong>Blue Stone Circle</strong>, the ultimate party band, rock to reggae to Top 40. Spanish Springs pulled out the big guns with <strong>Rocky and The Rollers</strong>, a Villages favorite for 27 years now. Sawgrass Grove had <strong>Zee-R Band</strong>, and Eastport's main stage gave us <strong>The Edge Of Rock</strong>. Five squares, five bands, one happy town.</p><p>I parked my cart at Lake Sumter and stayed put. No regrets.</p><h3>Saturday &amp; Sunday on the squares</h3><p>Saturday I bounced back to the lake for <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Voodoo Gypsies</a>, a classic rock cover band out of Central Florida. They play the good stuff, '60s through '80s, the songs you already know every word to. If you wanted to dance instead, <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood went full club mode</a> with CLUB CTRL featuring DJ Lu-S. Lights, beats, the works.</p><p>Sunday wound things down sweet. Lake Sumter brought back <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">September Dogs</a>, that female-fronted four-piece with the big layered vocals. And Brownwood closed the weekend with <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sheila &amp; The Sound</a>, running everything from Aretha to Journey. By the last song, half the square was singing along.</p><h3>Beat the heat</h3><p>Here's a little something worth knowing if you've been melting out there. The squares moved their summer hours. Through August 31, the bands now play <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">6:00 to 10:00 PM instead of 5:00 to 9:00</a>. Happy hour stays put at 5:00. Smart move, honestly. So if you've been skipping nights because the afternoon felt like a furnace, come a little later. The good stuff starts at six now.</p><p>And speaking of beating the heat, the bar huts are pouring a <strong>Tequila Sunrise</strong> this June while supplies last. Eight bucks for a single, ten-fifty for a double. I had one Saturday between sets. It did the job. You're welcome. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/bar-huts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bar huts</a>)</p><h3>Who-knew note</h3><p>For my catalogue folks always asking what to <em>do</em> around here. The POA has <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/18/poa-will-host-bingo-night-at-everglades-recreation-center/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bingo night coming up at the Everglades Recreation Center</a>. It's an easy, friendly way to meet a few neighbors if you're new in town. I've sent more than one new resident their way, and they always come back grinning. Tell 'em Susan sent you.</p><p>One more thing that made me smile. Villages-News ran a sweet piece on a couple here who <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/15/cant-believe-weve-been-married-70-years/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">just hit 70 years of marriage</a>. Seventy! That's the kind of story that reminds me why I love this place.</p><h3>The bottom line</h3><p>I'll be straight with you, since I always am. It was a quieter weekend on the news front. No big festival, no grand opening I could confirm. So this week the squares carried the show, and that's just fine by me. Some weekends, good music and a cold drink are the whole point.</p><p>If you missed it all, don't fret. The bands roll on every single night, six to ten, all summer long. Pick a square, grab a seat, and let somebody else do the singing.</p><p>See you around the square,<br>— Susan</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Villages Weekend Recap: Storms, Squares & a Steakhouse on the Way",
      "date": "June 15, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
      "body": "<p>Well, friends, summer showed up and brought the heat. Here's the scoop on the weekend that just wrapped.</p><p>It was a classic June stretch — sunshine in the morning, then those low-to-mid 90s and a few afternoon thunderstorms rolling through. Saturday topped out near the mid-90s with scattered storms, and Sunday wasn't far behind. (<a href=\"https://weather.com/weather/tenday/l/The+Villages+Florida+32162?canonicalCityId=b6bc2a6e7a7c3e05c21a8b5aabf350b7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">weather.com</a>) If the music felt like it started later than usual, you weren't imagining it. The squares are on summer hours — 6 to 10 p.m. now through August 31 — to dodge the worst of the heat. Happy hour still runs 5 to 6. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Villages Entertainment</a>)</p><h3>Music on the squares</h3><p>Brownwood is the one I can vouch for this weekend. Saturday night, <strong>DeadLanders</strong> took the Paddock Square stage — a DeLand band that leans country, classic and southern rock. Good boot-tapping stuff. Sunday, <strong>Beauty &amp; The Beats</strong> closed things out with a '70s, '80s and '90s mix — the legends, the chart-toppers, and a few one-hit wonders you forgot you loved. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood lineup</a>)</p><p>A little Susan honesty: the nightly calendars roll forward fast, so by Monday the Friday and Saturday names at Lake Sumter Landing and Spanish Springs had already cycled off the public page. Rather than guess, I'll point you to the live lineups so you can plan the week ahead — both <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lake Sumter Landing</a> and <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood</a> keep theirs updated. Easiest way to never miss your favorite band.</p><h3>A sip worth knowing</h3><p>Heading to the bar huts this month? The June feature is a <strong>Tequila Sunrise</strong> — $8 single, $10.50 double, while supplies last. Pretty in the cup and easy on a warm night. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/bar-huts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bar huts</a>) I sent my own neighbor for one Saturday. She approved.</p><h3>On the dining radar</h3><p>One for the foodies. A new spot is on the horizon at the Woodlands Championship Golf Course: <strong>Luxe Steakhouse &amp; Cellar</strong>, announced back in late March. It's the latest from Jack Suleiman, the same name behind RedSauce over at Lake Sumter Landing — so the bar's set high. No opening date yet (they say watch your Vmail), but steaks with a view of the greens? I'll be there. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillages.com/whats-new/shopping-dining/luxe-steakhouse-cellar-coming-soon/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Villages — Shopping &amp; Dining</a>)</p><h3>Who-knew note</h3><p>Saturday-morning treasure hunters had a good one. There was a neighborhood garage sale Friday and Saturday over in the <strong>Village of Calumet Grove</strong>, on SE 171st Horseshoe Lane, 7:30 to 12:30. (<a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Villages-News.com</a>) The early birds always get the good stuff — that's just the law of the land here. Watch the Villages-News listings and the Daily Sun calendar if you like a good Saturday rummage.</p><h3>The bottom line</h3><p>Quieter, hotter weekend on the squares — the kind where you sip something cold, catch a band after the sun drops, and let the storms pass. I kept this one to what I could actually confirm, because I'd rather tell you the truth than fill the page. There's a steakhouse coming, the music plays every night, and summer's just getting started.</p><p>Now go enjoy yourself.</p><p>See you around the square,<br>— Susan</p>"
    }
  ]
}